@page "/pixata-grid"
@using Pixata.Blazor.Components
@using Pixata.Blazor.Components.Grid
@using Pixata.Blazor.Sample.Models

<SitePageTitle>Pixata grid</SitePageTitle>

<h3>Pixata grid</h3>

<p><code>PixataGrid</code> is a grid that looks and behaves like the Telerik Blazor grid, without needing Telerik. It is being built up in stages (see <a href="https://github.com/MrYossu/Pixata.Utilities/issues/49">issue #49</a>), so this page will grow as features are added.</p>

<p>Each example shows a <code>PixataGrid</code> with a Telerik grid underneath it, bound to the same data with the same settings, so you can compare them.</p>

<div class="pixata-grid-sample-options">
  <label>
    Size
    <select @bind="_size">
      @foreach (PixataGridSize size in Enum.GetValues<PixataGridSize>()) {
        <option value="@size">@size</option>
      }
    </select>
  </label>
  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" @bind="_empty" />
    No data
  </label>
</div>

<h4>Declared columns</h4>

<p>The First name column has a width, Date of birth gets its format from a <code>[DisplayFormat]</code> attribute, Salary has a <code>DisplayFormat</code> and is right-aligned, and City is a nested field (<code>Address.City</code>). Columns without a <code>Title</code> get one from a <code>[Display(Name)]</code> attribute, or else from the field name split into words (so <code>DateOfBirth</code> becomes "Date of birth"). Telerik doesn't split the field name, so its headers differ there on purpose.</p>

<p class="pixata-grid-sample-label">PixataGrid</p>
<PixataGrid Data="Data" Size="_size" Height="300px">
  <GridColumns>
    <PixataGridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.FirstName)" Width="140px" />
    <PixataGridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.Surname)" />
    <PixataGridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.Email)" />
    <PixataGridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.DateOfBirth)" />
    <PixataGridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.Salary)" DisplayFormat="{0:C2}" TextAlign="PixataGridTextAlign.Right" />
    <PixataGridColumn Field="Address.City" Title="City" />
  </GridColumns>
</PixataGrid>

<p class="pixata-grid-sample-label">Telerik grid</p>
<TelerikGrid Data="Data" Size="@TelerikSize" Height="300px">
  <GridColumns>
    <GridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.FirstName)" Width="140px" />
    <GridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.Surname)" />
    <GridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.Email)" />
    <GridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.DateOfBirth)" />
    <GridColumn Field="@nameof(GridSampleEmployee.Salary)" DisplayFormat="{0:C2}" TextAlign="ColumnTextAlign.Right" />
    <GridColumn Field="Address.City" Title="City" />
  </GridColumns>
</TelerikGrid>

<h4>Auto-generated columns</h4>

<p>With <code>AutoGenerateColumns="true"</code> and no <code>GridColumns</code>, there's a column for each simple public property. Phone is left out by <code>[Display(AutoGenerateField = false)]</code>, and Address, being an object, is left out by <code>PixataGrid</code>. The grid has no height, so it is as tall as its rows.</p>

<p class="pixata-grid-sample-label">PixataGrid</p>
<PixataGrid Data="Data.Take(5)" Size="_size" AutoGenerateColumns="true" />

<p class="pixata-grid-sample-label">Telerik grid</p>
<TelerikGrid Data="Data.Take(5)" Size="@TelerikSize" AutoGenerateColumns="true" />

<style>
  .pixata-grid-sample-options {
    display: flex;
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .pixata-grid-sample-label {
    margin: 1rem 0 .25rem;
    font-weight: bold;
  }
</style>

@code {
  private readonly List<GridSampleEmployee> _employees = GridSampleEmployee.All();
  private PixataGridSize _size = PixataGridSize.Medium;
  private bool _empty;

  private List<GridSampleEmployee> Data =>
    _empty ? [] : _employees;

  private string TelerikSize => _size switch {
    PixataGridSize.Small => ThemeConstants.Grid.Size.Small,
    _ => ThemeConstants.Grid.Size.Medium
  };
}
